  "LESSONS": {
    "Sequences": [
      {
        "id": "l-seq",
        "title": "The nth term",
        "published": true,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "b-s1",
            "type": "slide",
            "title": "Sequence basics",
            "body": "A sequence is an ordered list of numbers, so position matters. Each number is a term, and the position tells you whether it is first, second, third, and so on.\n\nA term-to-term rule tells you how to move from one term to the next, such as \"add 4\". An nth-term rule, such as $4n + 1$, gives the term at position $n$ directly.\n\nWhen you meet a new sequence, check the gaps between consecutive terms. Do not just use the first gap, because some sequences have changing gaps."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-s2",
            "type": "mcq",
            "question": "In an nth-term rule such as $5n + 2$, what does $n$ represent?",
            "options": [
              "The common difference",
              "The total number of terms",
              "The position number",
              "The first term"
            ],
            "correct": 2,
            "explain": "$n$ is the position: $n = 1$ gives the first term, $n = 2$ the second, and so on."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-s3",
            "type": "slide",
            "title": "Using an nth-term formula",
            "body": "The letter $n$ means the position number, and in GCSE questions the first term is usually when $n = 1$. To find a term, substitute the position into the formula.\n\nFor the nth term $5n + 2$, substitute $n = 1$ and $n = 2$. This gives the first two terms:\n\n$$5(1) + 2 = 7$$\n\n$$5(2) + 2 = 12$$\n\nTo check whether 37 is in the sequence, set the formula equal to 37 and solve the equation:\n\n$$5n + 2 = 37$$\n\n$$5n = 35$$\n\n$$n = 7$$\n\nBecause $n = 7$ is a positive whole number, 37 is a term in the sequence. If you get a decimal, fraction, or negative value for $n$, the number is not a term."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-s4",
            "type": "mcq",
            "question": "What is the 4th term of the sequence $5n + 2$?",
            "options": [
              "20",
              "22",
              "27",
              "9"
            ],
            "correct": 1,
            "explain": "Substitute $n = 4$: $5(4) + 2 = 22$."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-s5",
            "type": "written",
            "question": "Is 52 a term in the sequence $5n + 2$? Show how you know.",
            "markScheme": "1 mark: sets up 5n + 2 = 52. 1 mark: solves to n = 10. 1 mark: concludes that 52 is a term (the 10th) because n is a positive whole number.",
            "marks": 3
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "Expanding brackets": [
      {
        "id": "l-expn",
        "kind": "notes",
        "title": "Notes: Expanding brackets",
        "published": true,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "b-n1",
            "type": "slide",
            "title": "Single brackets",
            "body": "Multiply the term outside by every term inside.\n\n$3(x + 4) = 3x + 12$"
          },
          {
            "id": "b-n2",
            "type": "slide",
            "title": "Double brackets",
            "body": "Use a grid or FOIL so every term meets every other term.\n\n$(x + 2)(x + 5) = x^2 + 7x + 10$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "l-exp",
        "title": "Squaring a bracket",
        "published": true,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "b-e1",
            "type": "video",
            "title": "Squaring brackets with the area model",
            "url": "",
            "note": "Watch this 6-minute video first. Pause it and try each example before the answer appears."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-e2",
            "type": "slide",
            "title": "Squaring a bracket",
            "body": "$(x + 3)^2$ means $(x + 3)(x + 3)$. Every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second.\n\nDraw a 2 by 2 grid. The four parts are $x^2$, $3x$, $3x$ and $9$. Add them up:\n\n$$(x + 3)^2 = x^2 + 6x + 9$$\n\nThe middle term is always double the product of the two terms in the bracket."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-e3",
            "type": "mcq",
            "question": "Which is equal to $(x + 5)^2$?",
            "options": [
              "$x^2 + 25$",
              "$x^2 + 10x + 25$",
              "$x^2 + 5x + 25$",
              "$2x + 10$"
            ],
            "correct": 1,
            "explain": "$(x + 5)(x + 5) = x^2 + 5x + 5x + 25 = x^2 + 10x + 25$."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-e4",
            "type": "slide",
            "title": "The most common mistake",
            "body": "Many students write $(a + b)^2 = a^2 + b^2$. This misses the two middle parts of the grid.\n\nCheck with numbers: $(1 + 2)^2 = 9$, but $1^2 + 2^2 = 5$. The difference is the missing $2ab$."
          },
          {
            "id": "b-e5",
            "type": "written",
            "question": "Explain why $(a + b)^2$ is not equal to $a^2 + b^2$.",
            "markScheme": "1 mark: writes (a + b)^2 as (a + b)(a + b) or uses an area model or a numerical counterexample. 1 mark: identifies the missing 2ab term.",
            "marks": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
